            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Conclusion First: Creative That Runs Well Doesn't Necessarily Sell Well</h2>
<p>A high click-through rate (CTR), fast ad spend, and impressive ECPM numbers—these are the "proof of success" most optimizers cite during reviews. But let me be blunt: <strong>these metrics only prove the creative "runs well," not that it "sells well."</strong></p>
<p>The difference between running well and selling well is fundamentally about traffic mindset versus conversion mindset. Creative that runs well excels at grabbing attention; creative that sells well excels at driving action. In actual campaigns, we've seen countless creatives with CTRs above 4% but ROAS below 1.5—budget spent, users clicked, but purchases nowhere to be found.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Why Your Creative "Runs but Doesn't Sell"?</h2>
<h3>1. Click ≠ Intent</h3>
<p>High CTR often means the creative is eye-catching, but the reason could be curiosity, sensationalism, or content irrelevant to your target audience. For example, a utility app once used "Test how many years your phone can still be used" as a headline and achieved 8% CTR—but nearly zero paying users. Users came purely out of curiosity, not genuine need.</p>
<h3>2. Traffic Quality ≠ Conversion Quality</h3>
<p>Platform algorithms prioritize displaying creatives to users most likely to click, not most likely to purchase, to maximize ad spend. This means creative data accumulated during the scaling phase is inherently biased, and using this data to guide future campaigns creates a vicious cycle.</p>
<h3>3. Short-Term Data ≠ Long-Term Value</h3>
<p>Some creatives perform brilliantly for three days but decay severely by day seven, with ROAS dropping from 2.0 to 0.8. Reviewing only early data leads to increased investment and massive losses.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Dimensions to Distinguish "Runs Well" vs "Sells Well"</h2>
<h3>Dimension 1: Evaluate Metric Combinations, Not Single Metrics</h3>
<p>Creative that runs well: CTR↑ CPM↓ ECPM↑<br />
Creative that sells well: CTR↑ CVR↑ CPA↓ ROAS↑ Paid user ratio↑</p>
<p><strong>Practical approach</strong>: Pull full funnel data—click to conversion to purchase—when reviewing. If CTR is high but CVR (conversion rate) is extremely low, the creative attracts clicks but lacks persuasion. Optimize CTA or landing page alignment.</p>
<h3>Dimension 2: Examine User Behavior Quality</h3>
<p>User quality varies dramatically across channels. A cross-border e-commerce team discovered that for creatives with identical CTRs, TikTok traffic had 60% lower average order value than Facebook traffic, with 40% higher return rates. This shows the same creative attracts vastly different quality users depending on the channel.</p>
<p><strong>Practical approach</strong>: Segment creative performance by traffic source. Focus on long-cycle metrics like paid conversion rate, time to first purchase, and 7-day LTV. If a creative brings massive downloads but few purchases, it's safe to label it "runs but doesn't sell."</p>
<h3>Dimension 3: Analyze Creative Lifecycle Curves</h3>
<p>Truly sellable creative shows stable or slowly rising ROAS curves (benefiting from model learning). Creative that runs but doesn't sell shows "high open, low close" curves—brilliant initial data, rapid decay within a week.</p>
<p><strong>Practical approach</strong>: Plot creative ROAS trends on a 7-day cycle to identify decay early. When A/B testing, prioritize creatives with stable ROAS curves, even if their initial CTR is lower than viral contenders.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Practical Recommendations: Build a Creative Grading Review System</h2>
<p>Stop judging creatives by CTR alone. Establish a creative review framework using these categories:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Exposure layer metrics</strong>: CTR, CPM, impressions</li>
<li><strong>Conversion layer metrics</strong>: CVR, click-to-registration rate, download-to-activation rate</li>
<li><strong>Monetization layer metrics</strong>: ROAS, CPA, ARPU, 7-day LTV</li>
<li><strong>Quality layer metrics</strong>: paid user ratio, time to first purchase, refund rate</li>
</ol>
<p>Only when both exposure and monetization layer metrics pass can you confirm the creative "runs and sells."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The core of creative review is escaping the "traffic illusion." Creative that runs well is a stepping stone; creative that sells well is a money printer. At Yiyu Digital Media, during ad creative diagnosis for clients, we've found that over 60% of creative optimization failures stem from treating "runs well" as a signal of "sells well." Next time you review, ask yourself first: Are the users this creative brings actually willing to pay?</p>
